\details{
Dominance is modelled as a deviation applied to heterozygotes (the het
indicator), with its share of phenotypic variance set by \code{prop}. In this
variance-partition grammar there is no separate "degree of dominance"
argument (there is no \verb{degree =}): the ratio of the \emph{scaled components'}
variances is \code{prop_dominance / prop_additive}, set through the layer
proportions. (A single degree-of-dominance scalar would be washed out by the
per-component variance scaling and is therefore not offered; for a per-locus
degree of dominance \code{d / abs(a)} use \code{additive(orthogonal = TRUE, a =, d =)}.)

\strong{Additive + dominance on the same loci is not orthogonal.} The dosage and
the heterozygote indicator covary by \eqn{-(2p - 1) 2pq} at each locus, so the
realized genetic variance is \verb{prop_A + prop_D + 2Cov(c_A, c_D)} for one
additive and one dominance layer (several layers of a type add their mutual
covariance to \code{Var(c_A)} or \code{Var(c_D)}) -- a structural,
allele-coding-dependent bias, not a finite-sample effect; its per-locus sign is
that of \verb{1 - 2p} for the counted-allele frequency \code{p}, so it is one-signed
across loci only when those frequencies are on one side of 0.5 -- and the
classical Va:Vd of the block differs from \code{prop_A:prop_D}. The object reports
the realized Var(A), Var(D) and 2Cov(A,D), and Var(c_A), Var(c_D) and
2Cov(c_A,c_D) (\verb{$ad_report}, and the note printed by \code{print()}); use \code{additive(orthogonal = TRUE, a =, d =)} for a
Fisher-orthogonal partition.

Dominance needs heterozygotes to be identifiable: it is identically zero at a
locus with no heterozygous individuals. If the selected loci carry none
(common on a near-inbred panel such as the bundled maize lines, ~0.4\%
heterozygous), \code{dominance()} \strong{errors} rather than silently substituting
loci; if only some of them carry none, it warns that the rest absorb \code{prop}.
Pre-filter to heterozygous markers with \code{filter_geno(hets = "include")},
fix het-bearing loci with \verb{qtn =}, or simulate dominance on an outbred or
F2-type population instead.

Under \code{architecture = "pleiotropy"} the dominance effects are drawn with the
same covariance as the additive layer (DECISION-023): shared loci carry
correlated effects and trait-specific loci independent ones, split by \code{pi},
so the dominance component targets \code{cor} -- its realized correlation
converges to \code{cor} as the loci and individuals grow, for loci in approximate
linkage equilibrium; see \code{\link[=simulate_phenotype]{simulate_phenotype()}} \code{cor} --
rather than every trait getting one identical effect series. With \code{same_as_add = TRUE} the
additive layer's shared and trait-specific loci are reused. Each locus's
effect is scaled by the realized standard deviation of its heterozygote
indicator. Fixing loci with \verb{qtn =} works under every architecture: under
"pleiotropy" every locus affects every trait (the same loci for each trait),
with an explicit \code{effect} setting the effects when no correlation is controlled;
under "ld" dominance reuses the additive layer's linked loci
(\code{same_as_add = TRUE}) or takes \code{qtn = list(trait1_loci, trait2_loci)} of
disjoint linked pairs (no marker may already be causal for the other trait in
an earlier layer), since a fresh random draw could make a marker causal for
both traits across layers.
